{
 "id": "2025-seo-storefront",
 "name": "Storefront Visibility and Streetscape Preference – AHP and Section Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Seo & Park (2025), International Journal of Urban Sciences, doi:10.1080/12265934.2025.2529477. Part 1: for six storefront components of Gyeongridan-gil, Seoul (signage utilization, opening utilization, signage ratio, opening ratio, colour scheme, material palette), respondents see one storefront edited into three types and answer 'Please check which type is more visible (perceived)?' for each of the three pairs (18 AHP comparisons). Part 2: six photomontage street sections (Left 1–3, Right 1–3) are rated for preference and perceived complexity on 7-point scales. The images are the authors' 2018 photographs from the paper's Tables 3 and 7, used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Original result (100 respondents): an inverted-U relation between preference and survey-rated complexity; Left 1 most preferred (4.06), Left 2 most complex (4.80) and least preferred (3.70). Differences from the original study: (1) images come from the paper's tables (type panels about 150 px each, sections about 1,250 px wide), not the original Google Form files; (2) the paper names a 5-point scale for the AHP items without its labels, so the template uses 'much more / more visible / equal' wording; (3) the 7-point scale end labels are not given in the paper and were added; (4) English wording follows the paper's translation of the Korean form.",
